lty of Mathematics and Computer Science, Nicolaus Copernicus University, Toruń, Poland."}, {"affiliation": "Institute of Advanced Studies, Nicolaus Copernicus University, Toruń, Poland."}]}}, {"fullName": "Szwed K", "lastName": "Szwed", "firstName": "Krzysztof", "initials": "K", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Faculty of Medicine, Bydgoszcz University of Science and Technology, Bydgoszcz, Poland."}]}}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "1", "volume": "29", "journalIssueId": 4291396, "dateOfPublication": "2027 Dec", "monthOfPublication": 12, "yearOfPublication": 2027, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Dialogues Clin Neurosci", "title": "Dialogues in clinical neuroscience", "essn": "1958-5969", "issn": "1294-8322", "nlmid": "101238198", "isoabbreviation": "Dialogues Clin Neurosci"}, "printPublicationDate": "2027-12-01"}, "pubYear": "2027", "pageInfo": "24-31", "abstractText": "<h4>Introduction</h4>Resting‑state fMRI is widely used to map brain connectivity, yet the field lacks a clear, data‑driven standard for acquisition duration. We compared traditional Single‑Band (SB) imaging with an accelerated Multiband factor 4 (MB4) protocol to identify a time‑efficient approach for capturing stable network topology.<h4>Methods</h4>Twenty‑five healthy young adults completed two sessions one month apart, each including counterbalanced 13‑minute SB and MB4 scans. Test-retest reliability was evaluated across multiple durations using Portrait Divergence, a multiscale graph‑theoretic metric. Supplementary analyses compared Portrait Divergence with the field-standard Intraclass Correlation Coefficient, assessed robustness across preprocessing pipelines and parcellation schemes, and examined MB4's ability to capture both cortical and subcortical connectivity.<h4>Results</h4>MB4 consistently outperformed SB across all tested durations, with peak topological stability at 9 minutes. Stability declined beyond this point, likely due to cumulative motion artefacts. Supplementary analyses confirmed MB4's robustness across preprocessing strategies and atlas choices and demonstrated effective whole‑brain coverage.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In healthy young adults, MB4 captures network topology more effectively than SB and reaches peak stability at 9 minutes before declining, challenging the assumption that longer scans are always better. These findings require validation in clinical, paediatric, and geriatric cohorts.", "affiliation": "Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, Nicolaus Copernicus University, Toruń, Poland.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "Nicolaus Copernicus University", "grantId": "Grant No. 90-SIDUB.6102.17.2023/DB5", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Brain", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "DG", "qualifierName": "diagnostic imaging"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "PH", "qualifierName": "physiology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Nerve Net", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "DG", "qualifierName": "diagnostic imaging"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "PH", "qualifierName": "physiology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Magnetic Resonance Imaging", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Brain Mapping", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Reproducibility of Results"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName